## Setup

```
just install
```

`codespell` and `prek` install via `just install`. `gitleaks` is a
standalone binary, install separately, pinned to match `prek.toml`:

```bash
GITLEAKS_VERSION={{ gitleaks_version }}
curl -sSL -o gitleaks.tar.gz "https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks/releases/download/v${GITLEAKS_VERSION}/gitleaks_${GITLEAKS_VERSION}_linux_x64.tar.gz"
tar xzf gitleaks.tar.gz gitleaks
sudo mv gitleaks /usr/local/bin/
rm gitleaks.tar.gz
gitleaks version
```

If you use VS Code, install the Python extension alongside the Ruff extension — otherwise Ruff-in-editor can silently fall back to a PATH/bundled binary instead of the `uv.lock`-pinned one in your venv.

## Toolkit

This template is toolkit-agnostic — no CLI/GUI dependency is pre-installed,
and `src/{{ project_slug }}/__main__.py` starts as a stub. Pick one and
`uv add` it yourself:

- **CLI:** `uv add typer` (recommended over Click — type-hint-driven,
  matches this template's `mypy --strict` setup, and gives you `--help`/
  completion generation for free). Wire your `app` object up in
  `__main__.py`, subcommands in `commands/` (thin, delegates to `core/`
  for actual logic).
- **GUI:** `tkinter` needs no install (stdlib) — for anything else,
  `uv add pygame`, `uv add pyside6`, etc. as needed. Build your mainloop
  in `__main__.py`; `commands/` doesn't apply here, delete it and keep
  testable logic in `core/`.

`[project.scripts]` in `pyproject.toml` already points `{{ project_slug }}`
at `{{ project_slug }}.__main__:main` — nothing to change there once you've
picked a toolkit.

## Git hooks

```
just install-hooks    # one-time: installs prek's git hook shims (see prek.toml)
```

Runs formatting, type checks, and complexity checks automatically before
each commit, plus commit message / branch-name conventions (commitizen),
generic file hygiene, spell checking, and secret scanning. Install this
once per clone.

## Dev commands

```
just format           # ruff check --fix + ruff format
just check-format     # ruff format --check (non-mutating)
just check-types      # mypy
just check-complexity # complexipy
just check-spelling      # codespell
just check-secrets       # gitleaks, staged changes only — run before committing
just check-versions-sync # gitleaks pin in prek.toml vs. installed binary
just test                # pytest + coverage
just check               # format + types + complexity + spelling + version-sync + test bundle
```

`check-secrets` is deliberately not part of the `just check` bundle — it
only makes sense once something's staged. Run it (or rely on the
`install-hooks` git hook) before every commit.

`codespell`, like `ruff`/`mypy`/`complexipy`, is a venv dependency — its
pre-commit hook just runs `just check-spelling`, so there's a single pin
(`pyproject.toml`) and nothing to drift. `gitleaks` is a standalone binary
outside the venv, pinned separately in `prek.toml` and the README install
snippet above; `just check-versions-sync` (also wired in as a prek hook)
checks the installed binary against that pin so the two can't silently
drift apart.
